  {"id":"ss-3","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What time course is typical for serotonin syndrome onset after a serotonergic agent dose change?","answer":"Most cases present within 6-24 hours of a serotonergic agent dose change or addition of a new serotonergic agent","rationale":"Rapid onset reflects direct pharmacological effect of excess serotonin at 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors, unlike NMS which takes days to develop.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"StatPearls: StatPearls   Serotonin Syndrome","page":6}],"confusable_with":"NMS"},
  {"id":"ss-4","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What life-threatening complications can occur in severe serotonin syndrome?","answer":"Intravascular coagulation with platelet abnormalities, rhabdomyolysis with highly elevated CK, and metabolic acidosis","rationale":"Severe hyperthermia and muscle rigidity in serotonin syndrome cause rhabdomyolysis and DIC through the same mechanism as other hyperthermic syndromes.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"StatPearls: StatPearls   Serotonin Syndrome","page":6}],"confusable_with":""},
  {"id":"ss-5","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome","domain":"Internal medicine: emergency & acute care","discipline":"medicine","stem":"What drug class can be used in the management of carcinoid syndrome and vascular headaches due to its serotonin antagonist activity?","answer":"Cyproheptadine has significant serotonin antagonist activity; used in Cushing disease, carcinoid syndrome, and vascular (cluster) headaches","rationale":"Cyproheptadine is a first-generation antihistamine with 5-HT2 receptor antagonism; it is also used as rescue therapy in serotonin syndrome.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":443}],"confusable_with":""},
